window.__programmeIndexChunk("statements",{"62da37b5b0836df21cb0bcdb223688c950bc2103845de5293045c0bbd7f60149":{"markdown":"**Lemma 1.B (affine denominators in derived maps).** Let \\(V=\\operatorname{Spec}A\\), \\(W=\\bigcup_{i=1}^rD(f_i)\\), and let \\(P\\) be perfect on \\(V\\). Every derived map \\(P|_W\\to E|_W\\), with \\(E\\in D_{\\mathrm{QCoh}}(V)\\), is represented by\n\\[\nP\\xleftarrow{\\beta}P\\otimes^{\\mathbf L}I\\xrightarrow{\\gamma}E,\n\\tag{1.B}\n\\]\nwhere \\(I\\) is perfect, has a map \\(I\\to\\mathcal O_V\\), and this map, hence \\(\\beta\\), is an isomorphism on \\(W\\).","html":"<p><strong>Lemma 1.B (affine denominators in derived maps).<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V=\\operatorname{Spec}A\\)\">\\(V=\\operatorname{Spec}A\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W=\\bigcup_{i=1}^rD(f_i)\\)\">\\(W=\\bigcup_{i=1}^rD(f_i)\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> be perfect on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span>. Every derived map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P|_W\\to E|_W\\)\">\\(P|_W\\to E|_W\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\in D_{\\mathrm{QCoh}}(V)\\)\">\\(E\\in D_{\\mathrm{QCoh}}(V)\\)<\/span>, is represented by\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nP\\xleftarrow{\\beta}P\\otimes^{\\mathbf L}I\\xrightarrow{\\gamma}E,\n\\tag{1.B}\n\\]\">\\[\nP\\xleftarrow{\\beta}P\\otimes^{\\mathbf L}I\\xrightarrow{\\gamma}E,\n\\tag{1.B}\n\\]<\/span>\nwhere <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> is perfect, has a map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\to\\mathcal O_V\\)\">\\(I\\to\\mathcal O_V\\)<\/span>, and this map, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta\\)\">\\(\\beta\\)<\/span>, is an isomorphism on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/AG-QC/src/the-right-adjoint-of-derived-pushforward.md","start_offset":4890,"end_offset":5324,"label":"Lemma 1.B","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"620b327a753d209aee3f7294fe2eaa701c6630096683e71c69537a9a843704db":{"markdown":"**Exercise 8.4 — Equivalence of spectral measures.** Show that two group representations are unitarily equivalent if and only if their SNAG PVMs are unitarily equivalent.","html":"<p><strong>Exercise 8.4 — Equivalence of spectral measures.<\/strong> Show that two group representations are unitarily equivalent if and only if their SNAG PVMs are unitarily equivalent.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/HA-LCA/src/unitary-representations-of-abelian-groups-the-spectral-theorem.md","start_offset":45295,"end_offset":45467,"label":"Exercise 8.4","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"6233af2ab5dfa6383e9043f51278f47bd8b42cadc8a878e8815a1dad3d747b7e":{"markdown":"**Corollary 3.3 — Synthesis on a small boundary.** If \\(\\partial E\\) contains no nonempty perfect set, then the closed set \\(E\\) admits synthesis. This includes finite sets, closed discrete sets, and sets that are both closed and open. Also, for every closed ideal \\(I\\),\n\\[\n \\widehat f=0\\text{ on a neighbourhood of }\\nu(I)\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f\\in I.                        \\tag{13}\n\\]\nThe minimal ideal is \\(j(E)\\), as described in (10)–(11).","html":"<p><strong>Corollary 3.3 — Synthesis on a small boundary.<\/strong> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\partial E\\)\">\\(\\partial E\\)<\/span> contains no nonempty perfect set, then the closed set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> admits synthesis. This includes finite sets, closed discrete sets, and sets that are both closed and open. Also, for every closed ideal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widehat f=0\\text{ on a neighbourhood of }\\nu(I)\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f\\in I.                        \\tag{13}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widehat f=0\\text{ on a neighbourhood of }\\nu(I)\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f\\in I.                        \\tag{13}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe minimal ideal is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j(E)\\)\">\\(j(E)\\)<\/span>, as described in (10)–(11).<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/HA-LCA/src/tauberian-theorems-and-spectral-synthesis.md","start_offset":24966,"end_offset":25420,"label":"Corollary 3.3","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"62b91a2dfcc6c9559836b8b529d8d5396eb1c7866887def35d0ac22afa9ea31d":{"markdown":"**Proposition 3.1.** Multiplication gives faithful spatial tensor identifications\n\\[\nC^*(\\operatorname{CAR}(L),\\operatorname{CAR}(N)_{\\rm ev})\n\\cong\\operatorname{CAR}(L)\\otimes_{\\min}\\operatorname{CAR}(N)_{\\rm ev},\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nand the analogous identification with the first leg even.","html":"<p><strong>Proposition 3.1.<\/strong> Multiplication gives faithful spatial tensor identifications\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nC^*(\\operatorname{CAR}(L),\\operatorname{CAR}(N)_{\\rm ev})\n\\cong\\operatorname{CAR}(L)\\otimes_{\\min}\\operatorname{CAR}(N)_{\\rm ev},\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\">\\[\nC^*(\\operatorname{CAR}(L),\\operatorname{CAR}(N)_{\\rm ev})\n\\cong\\operatorname{CAR}(L)\\otimes_{\\min}\\operatorname{CAR}(N)_{\\rm ev},\n\\tag{10}\n\\]<\/span>\nand the analogous identification with the first leg even.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-APPROX/src/car-fock-and-quasifree-states.md","start_offset":13298,"end_offset":13584,"label":"Proposition 3.1","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"620a07674eead8b8e566d3d2431c993dc528bf555dd6508a86b174bad786698f":{"markdown":"**Lemma 4.1.** Let \\(x\\) be supported by a finite set \\(B\\subset G\\). Suppose \\(g_0,g_1\\in G\\) have the property\n\\[\nr^{-1}g_1r'=g_1,\\quad r,r'\\in Bg_0^{-1}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad r=r'.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nThen the following coefficient is a nonnegative real number and satisfies\n\\[\n|x(g_0)|^2\\le (x^**\\delta_{g_1}*x)(g_0^{-1}g_1g_0).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]","html":"<p><strong>Lemma 4.1.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> be supported by a finite set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\subset G\\)\">\\(B\\subset G\\)<\/span>. Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_0,g_1\\in G\\)\">\\(g_0,g_1\\in G\\)<\/span> have the property\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nr^{-1}g_1r&#x27;=g_1,\\quad r,r&#x27;\\in Bg_0^{-1}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad r=r&#x27;.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\">\\[\nr^{-1}g_1r&#x27;=g_1,\\quad r,r&#x27;\\in Bg_0^{-1}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad r=r&#x27;.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]<\/span>\nThen the following coefficient is a nonnegative real number and satisfies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n|x(g_0)|^2\\le (x^**\\delta_{g_1}*x)(g_0^{-1}g_1g_0).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\">\\[\n|x(g_0)|^2\\le (x^**\\delta_{g_1}*x)(g_0^{-1}g_1g_0).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-APPROX/src/group-masas-and-singular-affine-examples.md","start_offset":7004,"end_offset":7346,"label":"Lemma 4.1","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"62eab9cb928467f72f9f2bd6c26eadef639af5ebe30acd427925cbd20d057582":{"markdown":"**Exercise 5.** Show that the dyadic reconstruction in Lemma 3 can be passed through any isometric action filter, and explain why mere pointwise frequency convergence would not suffice.","html":"<p><strong>Exercise 5.<\/strong> Show that the dyadic reconstruction in Lemma 3 can be passed through any isometric action filter, and explain why mere pointwise frequency convergence would not suffice.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L35.md","start_offset":43885,"end_offset":44072,"label":"Exercise 5","extraction_boundary":"all_numbered_statements_in_bound_passage"},"622c4a1ab797eb0df0041b5b126be5603a6bafa5dbb144c2c42f6deeee86044f":{"markdown":"**Exercise.** Why is the compact annulus in (Z4) twice as wide as the final additive gap? **Solution.** The low-band implementer has spectrum in \\([-R/2,R/2]\\). The two matrix factors in (Z20)–(Z22) can together shift a high frequency by at most \\(R\\). Frequencies initially outside \\((-2R,2R)\\) therefore stay outside \\((-R,R)\\), while the whole low algebra is fixed by the corrected action.","html":"<p><strong>Exercise.<\/strong> Why is the compact annulus in (Z4) twice as wide as the final additive gap? <strong>Solution.<\/strong> The low-band implementer has spectrum in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([-R/2,R/2]\\)\">\\([-R/2,R/2]\\)<\/span>. The two matrix factors in (Z20)–(Z22) can together shift a high frequency by at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span>. Frequencies initially outside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((-2R,2R)\\)\">\\((-2R,2R)\\)<\/span> therefore stay outside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((-R,R)\\)\">\\((-R,R)\\)<\/span>, while the whole low algebra is fixed by the corrected action.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-Z.md","start_offset":22120,"end_offset":22514,"label":null,"extraction_boundary":"all_numbered_statements_in_bound_passage"},"625ece16efb2127224f38b8f47c5b8f3319f64e0952545d9a6762fc34739654f":{"markdown":"**Theorem.** Every everywhere-defined complex-linear derivation \\(d:M\\to M\\) of an arbitrary von Neumann algebra is ultraweakly continuous. This conclusion does not require an implementer, a faithful state, or a separability hypothesis.\n\nWe use three precise earlier facts. [L34, Theorem 4.3](OA-FLOW-L34.md#oa-flow.dfd.algebra) proves automatic boundedness for an everywhere-defined derivation on any C*-algebra, including a nonunital one. [CP-6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.6) identifies \\(M_*\\) isometrically with a Banach subspace of \\(M^*\\), proves that this subspace is norm closed, and identifies its elements exactly with the ultraweakly continuous linear functionals. The ultraweak topology is \\(\\sigma(M,M_*)\\). Finally, [NF-6](OA-FLOW-NF.md#oa-flow.nf.6) proves that a positive linear map between arbitrary von Neumann algebras is ultraweakly continuous if and only if it preserves bounded increasing positive suprema. Its scalar input is the complete [NF-1–4 proof for bounded positive functionals](OA-FLOW-NF.md#oa-flow.nf.1), not an assumption about derivations.\n\n**Exponentiating a bounded star derivation.** First suppose\n\\[\n d(x^*)=d(x)^*.\n \\tag{N1}\n\\]\nBy L34 the operator \\(d\\) is bounded. The zero algebra has only the zero map, which is ultraweakly continuous; suppose henceforth that \\(M\\ne0\\). Its identity satisfies \\(d(1)=2d(1)\\), so \\(d(1)=0\\). For real \\(t\\), define in the Banach algebra of bounded linear maps on \\(M\\)\n\\[\n \\alpha_t=\\exp(td)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{t^n}{n!}d^n,\n \\qquad\n \\|\\alpha_t-I\\|\\leq e^{|t|\\|d\\|}-1.\n \\tag{N2}\n\\]\nThe series converges absolutely in operator norm. Here \\(I\\) is the identity map and \\(d^0=I\\).\n\nRepeated use of the derivation rule gives\n\\[\n d^n(xy)=\\sum_{j=0}^{n}\\binom nj d^j(x)d^{n-j}(y).\n \\tag{N3}\n\\]\nFor completeness, applying \\(d\\) to each product in the formula for \\(n\\) gives two sums; terms of a fixed new index combine by\n\\(\\binom nj+\\binom n{j-1}=\\binom{n+1}j\\). This proves (N3) by induction, starting with \\(n=0\\). The double series\n\\[\n \\sum_{j,k\\geq0}\\frac{t^{j+k}}{j!\\,k!}d^j(x)d^k(y)\n \\tag{N4}\n\\]\nis absolutely convergent, since the sum of the norms is at most\n\\(e^{2|t|\\|d\\|}\\|x\\|\\|y\\|\\).\nRegrouping by \\(j+k\\), and using (N3), proves\n\\(\\alpha_t(xy)=\\alpha_t(x)\\alpha_t(y)\\).\nReal coefficients and (N1) give\n\\(\\alpha_t(x^*)=\\alpha_t(x)^*\\), and \\(d(1)=0\\) gives \\(\\alpha_t(1)=1\\).\nThe same absolutely convergent product calculation in the algebra of bounded maps gives\n\\[\n \\alpha_s\\alpha_t=\\alpha_{s+t},\n \\qquad \\alpha_0=I,\\qquad\n \\alpha_t^{-1}=\\alpha_{-t}.\n \\tag{N5}\n\\]\nThus every \\(\\alpha_t\\) is a unital star automorphism.\n\nBoth \\(\\alpha_t\\) and its inverse are positive: a positive element is a square \\(x^*x\\), and multiplicativity preserves that expression. In particular,\n\\[\n 0\\leq \\alpha_t(x)^*\\alpha_t(x)\n       =\\alpha_t(x^*x)\\leq\\|x\\|^2 1\n\\]\ngives \\(\\|\\alpha_t(x)\\|\\leq\\|x\\|\\). Applying the same inequality to the inverse gives equality. Hence \\(\\|\\alpha_t\\|=1\\), and (N2) together with (N5) gives operator-norm continuity at every time.\n\n**From order preservation to the predual.** Fix a real \\(t\\), and let \\(a_\\lambda\\uparrow a\\) be a bounded increasing positive net in \\(M\\), with \\(a\\) its supremum. Positivity makes \\(\\alpha_t(a)\\) an upper bound of all \\(\\alpha_t(a_\\lambda)\\). If \\(b\\) is another self-adjoint upper bound, the order-preserving inverse gives\n\\(\\alpha_{-t}(b)\\geq a_\\lambda\\) for every \\(\\lambda\\).\nThe least-upper-bound property implies \\(\\alpha_{-t}(b)\\geq a\\), hence \\(b\\geq\\alpha_t(a)\\). Therefore\n\\[\n \\sup_\\lambda \\alpha_t(a_\\lambda)\n       =\\alpha_t\\!\\left(\\sup_\\lambda a_\\lambda\\right).\n \\tag{N6}\n\\]\nThis is an order argument for arbitrary nets.\n\nThe passage from (N6) to ultraweak continuity is precisely the proved NF-6 criterion. To see the role of its functional input, take \\(\\omega\\in M_*^+\\). The functional \\(\\omega\\circ\\alpha_t\\) is bounded and positive. By (N6) and the order-normality of positive predual functionals, it preserves bounded increasing positive suprema. NF-1–4 therefore put it in \\(M_*\\). NF-6 also proves explicitly, by polarization of the CP vector series, that every member of \\(M_*\\) is a complex linear combination of four positive members. Consequently\n\\[\n \\omega\\circ\\alpha_t\\in M_*\n       \\qquad(\\omega\\in M_*,\\ t\\in\\mathbb R).\n \\tag{N7}\n\\]\nSince the functionals in \\(M_*\\) define the ultraweak topology, (N7) proves that \\(\\alpha_t\\) is ultraweakly continuous on all of \\(M\\). Applying this to \\(-t\\) proves that it is an ultraweak homeomorphism. No identification of order continuity with ultraweak continuity has been left unproved.\n\n**Differentiating inside the closed predual.** For \\(t\\ne0\\), put\n\\[\n T_t=\\frac{\\alpha_t-I}{t}.\n \\tag{N8}\n\\]\nEquation (N7) and linearity of \\(M_*\\) imply\n\\(\\omega\\circ T_t\\in M_*\\) for every \\(\\omega\\in M_*\\).\nThe exponential remainder gives the quantitative operator-norm estimate\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|T_t-d\\|\n &\\leq \\sum_{n=2}^{\\infty}\n              \\frac{|t|^{n-1}\\|d\\|^n}{n!}\\\\\n &\\leq \\frac{|t|\\|d\\|^2}{2}e^{|t|\\|d\\|}\n       \\longrightarrow0\\qquad(t\\longrightarrow0).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{N9}\n\\]\nThe second inequality follows by writing \\(n=k+2\\) and using\n\\((k+2)!\\geq2k!\\); it also covers \\(d=0\\).\nFor each fixed \\(\\omega\\in M_*\\),\n\\[\n \\|\\omega\\circ T_t-\\omega\\circ d\\|_{M^*}\n       \\leq\\|\\omega\\|\\|T_t-d\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{N10}\n\\]\nThe norm-closedness proved in CP-6 now gives\n\\(\\omega\\circ d\\in M_*\\).\nFor an arbitrary ultraweakly convergent net \\(x_\\lambda\\to x\\), it follows that\n\\[\n \\omega(d(x_\\lambda))\n       =(\\omega\\circ d)(x_\\lambda)\n       \\longrightarrow(\\omega\\circ d)(x)\n       =\\omega(d(x))\n       \\qquad(\\omega\\in M_*).\n \\tag{N11}\n\\]\nThus \\(d(x_\\lambda)\\to d(x)\\) ultraweakly. There is no interchange of the \\(t\\)-limit with the \\(\\lambda\\)-net: (N10) first places each fixed pulled-back functional in the predual, and (N11) then uses that functional.\n\n**The general complex derivation.** For an arbitrary \\(d\\), L34 again gives boundedness. Set\n\\[\n d^\\dagger(x)=d(x^*)^*,\\qquad\n d_1=\\frac{d+d^\\dagger}{2},\\qquad\n d_2=\\frac{d-d^\\dagger}{2i}.\n \\tag{N12}\n\\]\nThe two conjugations make \\(d^\\dagger\\) complex linear, and reversing the product twice gives\n\\[\n d^\\dagger(xy)\n       =x\\,d^\\dagger(y)+d^\\dagger(x)y.\n \\tag{N13}\n\\]\nThus it is a derivation. Moreover\n\\((d^\\dagger)^\\dagger=d\\), and the dagger operation conjugates scalar coefficients. These identities give\n\\(d_j^\\dagger=d_j\\) for \\(j=1,2\\); both \\(d_j\\) are star derivations. The isometry of star also gives\n\\(\\|d^\\dagger\\|=\\|d\\|\\), hence \\(\\|d_j\\|\\leq\\|d\\|\\).\nThe star case makes both \\(d_1\\) and \\(d_2\\) ultraweakly continuous. Their sum\n\\[\n d=d_1+i d_2\n \\tag{N14}\n\\]\nis therefore ultraweakly continuous, proving the theorem. \\(\\square\\)\n\nThe exponential product argument (N2)–(N5) also works in a possibly nonunital C*-algebra: \\(I\\) denotes its identity linear map, and the statements involving an algebra identity are simply omitted. L34's boundedness theorem already covers that convention. The order and predual part above concerns the von Neumann algebra \\(M\\), with its own identity and its specified predual; the zero algebra was handled separately.\n\nThis proof establishes normality before any inner implementer is chosen. The direct vector-series proof after the innerness theorem remains useful: once \\(d(x)=kx-xk\\) is known, it proves the same continuity by substituting vectors in the two multiplication maps. The present argument gives an independent order-and-predual route to that conclusion.\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.gi.representation-example\"><\/a>","html":"<p><strong>Theorem.<\/strong> Every everywhere-defined complex-linear derivation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d:M\\to M\\)\">\\(d:M\\to M\\)<\/span> of an arbitrary von Neumann algebra is ultraweakly continuous. This conclusion does not require an implementer, a faithful state, or a separability hypothesis.<\/p>\n<p>We use three precise earlier facts. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L34.html#oa-flow.dfd.algebra\">L34, Theorem 4.3<\/a> proves automatic boundedness for an everywhere-defined derivation on any C*-algebra, including a nonunital one. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6\">CP-6<\/a> identifies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span> isometrically with a Banach subspace of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M^*\\)\">\\(M^*\\)<\/span>, proves that this subspace is norm closed, and identifies its elements exactly with the ultraweakly continuous linear functionals. The ultraweak topology is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma(M,M_*)\\)\">\\(\\sigma(M,M_*)\\)<\/span>. Finally, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NF.html#oa-flow.nf.6\">NF-6<\/a> proves that a positive linear map between arbitrary von Neumann algebras is ultraweakly continuous if and only if it preserves bounded increasing positive suprema. Its scalar input is the complete <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NF.html#oa-flow.nf.1\">NF-1–4 proof for bounded positive functionals<\/a>, not an assumption about derivations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exponentiating a bounded star derivation.<\/strong> First suppose\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n d(x^*)=d(x)^*.\n \\tag{N1}\n\\]\">\\[\n d(x^*)=d(x)^*.\n \\tag{N1}\n\\]<\/span>\nBy L34 the operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span> is bounded. The zero algebra has only the zero map, which is ultraweakly continuous; suppose henceforth that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\ne0\\)\">\\(M\\ne0\\)<\/span>. Its identity satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d(1)=2d(1)\\)\">\\(d(1)=2d(1)\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d(1)=0\\)\">\\(d(1)=0\\)<\/span>. For real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>, define in the Banach algebra of bounded linear maps on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\alpha_t=\\exp(td)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{t^n}{n!}d^n,\n \\qquad\n \\|\\alpha_t-I\\|\\leq e^{|t|\\|d\\|}-1.\n \\tag{N2}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\alpha_t=\\exp(td)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{t^n}{n!}d^n,\n \\qquad\n \\|\\alpha_t-I\\|\\leq e^{|t|\\|d\\|}-1.\n \\tag{N2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe series converges absolutely in operator norm. Here <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> is the identity map and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d^0=I\\)\">\\(d^0=I\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Repeated use of the derivation rule gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n d^n(xy)=\\sum_{j=0}^{n}\\binom nj d^j(x)d^{n-j}(y).\n \\tag{N3}\n\\]\">\\[\n d^n(xy)=\\sum_{j=0}^{n}\\binom nj d^j(x)d^{n-j}(y).\n \\tag{N3}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor completeness, applying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span> to each product in the formula for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> gives two sums; terms of a fixed new index combine by\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\binom nj+\\binom n{j-1}=\\binom{n+1}j\\)\">\\(\\binom nj+\\binom n{j-1}=\\binom{n+1}j\\)<\/span>. This proves (N3) by induction, starting with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n=0\\)\">\\(n=0\\)<\/span>. The double series\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\sum_{j,k\\geq0}\\frac{t^{j+k}}{j!\\,k!}d^j(x)d^k(y)\n \\tag{N4}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\sum_{j,k\\geq0}\\frac{t^{j+k}}{j!\\,k!}d^j(x)d^k(y)\n \\tag{N4}\n\\]<\/span>\nis absolutely convergent, since the sum of the norms is at most\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{2|t|\\|d\\|}\\|x\\|\\|y\\|\\)\">\\(e^{2|t|\\|d\\|}\\|x\\|\\|y\\|\\)<\/span>.\nRegrouping by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j+k\\)\">\\(j+k\\)<\/span>, and using (N3), proves\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t(xy)=\\alpha_t(x)\\alpha_t(y)\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t(xy)=\\alpha_t(x)\\alpha_t(y)\\)<\/span>.\nReal coefficients and (N1) give\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t(x^*)=\\alpha_t(x)^*\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t(x^*)=\\alpha_t(x)^*\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d(1)=0\\)\">\\(d(1)=0\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t(1)=1\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t(1)=1\\)<\/span>.\nThe same absolutely convergent product calculation in the algebra of bounded maps gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\alpha_s\\alpha_t=\\alpha_{s+t},\n \\qquad \\alpha_0=I,\\qquad\n \\alpha_t^{-1}=\\alpha_{-t}.\n \\tag{N5}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\alpha_s\\alpha_t=\\alpha_{s+t},\n \\qquad \\alpha_0=I,\\qquad\n \\alpha_t^{-1}=\\alpha_{-t}.\n \\tag{N5}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t\\)<\/span> is a unital star automorphism.<\/p>\n<p>Both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t\\)<\/span> and its inverse are positive: a positive element is a square <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x^*x\\)\">\\(x^*x\\)<\/span>, and multiplicativity preserves that expression. In particular,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n 0\\leq \\alpha_t(x)^*\\alpha_t(x)\n       =\\alpha_t(x^*x)\\leq\\|x\\|^2 1\n\\]\">\\[\n 0\\leq \\alpha_t(x)^*\\alpha_t(x)\n       =\\alpha_t(x^*x)\\leq\\|x\\|^2 1\n\\]<\/span>\ngives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\alpha_t(x)\\|\\leq\\|x\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\alpha_t(x)\\|\\leq\\|x\\|\\)<\/span>. Applying the same inequality to the inverse gives equality. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\alpha_t\\|=1\\)\">\\(\\|\\alpha_t\\|=1\\)<\/span>, and (N2) together with (N5) gives operator-norm continuity at every time.<\/p>\n<p><strong>From order preservation to the predual.<\/strong> Fix a real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_\\lambda\\uparrow a\\)\">\\(a_\\lambda\\uparrow a\\)<\/span> be a bounded increasing positive net in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span> its supremum. Positivity makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t(a)\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t(a)\\)<\/span> an upper bound of all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t(a_\\lambda)\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t(a_\\lambda)\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span> is another self-adjoint upper bound, the order-preserving inverse gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_{-t}(b)\\geq a_\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\alpha_{-t}(b)\\geq a_\\lambda\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span>.\nThe least-upper-bound property implies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_{-t}(b)\\geq a\\)\">\\(\\alpha_{-t}(b)\\geq a\\)<\/span>, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\geq\\alpha_t(a)\\)\">\\(b\\geq\\alpha_t(a)\\)<\/span>. Therefore\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\sup_\\lambda \\alpha_t(a_\\lambda)\n       =\\alpha_t\\!\\left(\\sup_\\lambda a_\\lambda\\right).\n \\tag{N6}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\sup_\\lambda \\alpha_t(a_\\lambda)\n       =\\alpha_t\\!\\left(\\sup_\\lambda a_\\lambda\\right).\n \\tag{N6}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is an order argument for arbitrary nets.<\/p>\n<p>The passage from (N6) to ultraweak continuity is precisely the proved NF-6 criterion. To see the role of its functional input, take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\in M_*^+\\)\">\\(\\omega\\in M_*^+\\)<\/span>. The functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\circ\\alpha_t\\)\">\\(\\omega\\circ\\alpha_t\\)<\/span> is bounded and positive. By (N6) and the order-normality of positive predual functionals, it preserves bounded increasing positive suprema. NF-1–4 therefore put it in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span>. NF-6 also proves explicitly, by polarization of the CP vector series, that every member of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span> is a complex linear combination of four positive members. Consequently\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\omega\\circ\\alpha_t\\in M_*\n       \\qquad(\\omega\\in M_*,\\ t\\in\\mathbb R).\n \\tag{N7}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\omega\\circ\\alpha_t\\in M_*\n       \\qquad(\\omega\\in M_*,\\ t\\in\\mathbb R).\n \\tag{N7}\n\\]<\/span>\nSince the functionals in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span> define the ultraweak topology, (N7) proves that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha_t\\)\">\\(\\alpha_t\\)<\/span> is ultraweakly continuous on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>. Applying this to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-t\\)\">\\(-t\\)<\/span> proves that it is an ultraweak homeomorphism. No identification of order continuity with ultraweak continuity has been left unproved.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Differentiating inside the closed predual.<\/strong> For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\ne0\\)\">\\(t\\ne0\\)<\/span>, put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T_t=\\frac{\\alpha_t-I}{t}.\n \\tag{N8}\n\\]\">\\[\n T_t=\\frac{\\alpha_t-I}{t}.\n \\tag{N8}\n\\]<\/span>\nEquation (N7) and linearity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span> imply\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\circ T_t\\in M_*\\)\">\\(\\omega\\circ T_t\\in M_*\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\in M_*\\)\">\\(\\omega\\in M_*\\)<\/span>.\nThe exponential remainder gives the quantitative operator-norm estimate\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|T_t-d\\|\n &amp;\\leq \\sum_{n=2}^{\\infty}\n              \\frac{|t|^{n-1}\\|d\\|^n}{n!}\\\\\n &amp;\\leq \\frac{|t|\\|d\\|^2}{2}e^{|t|\\|d\\|}\n       \\longrightarrow0\\qquad(t\\longrightarrow0).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{N9}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\|T_t-d\\|\n &amp;\\leq \\sum_{n=2}^{\\infty}\n              \\frac{|t|^{n-1}\\|d\\|^n}{n!}\\\\\n &amp;\\leq \\frac{|t|\\|d\\|^2}{2}e^{|t|\\|d\\|}\n       \\longrightarrow0\\qquad(t\\longrightarrow0).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{N9}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe second inequality follows by writing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n=k+2\\)\">\\(n=k+2\\)<\/span> and using\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((k+2)!\\geq2k!\\)\">\\((k+2)!\\geq2k!\\)<\/span>; it also covers <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d=0\\)\">\\(d=0\\)<\/span>.\nFor each fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\in M_*\\)\">\\(\\omega\\in M_*\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|\\omega\\circ T_t-\\omega\\circ d\\|_{M^*}\n       \\leq\\|\\omega\\|\\|T_t-d\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{N10}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|\\omega\\circ T_t-\\omega\\circ d\\|_{M^*}\n       \\leq\\|\\omega\\|\\|T_t-d\\|\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{N10}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe norm-closedness proved in CP-6 now gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\circ d\\in M_*\\)\">\\(\\omega\\circ d\\in M_*\\)<\/span>.\nFor an arbitrary ultraweakly convergent net <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_\\lambda\\to x\\)\">\\(x_\\lambda\\to x\\)<\/span>, it follows that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\omega(d(x_\\lambda))\n       =(\\omega\\circ d)(x_\\lambda)\n       \\longrightarrow(\\omega\\circ d)(x)\n       =\\omega(d(x))\n       \\qquad(\\omega\\in M_*).\n \\tag{N11}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\omega(d(x_\\lambda))\n       =(\\omega\\circ d)(x_\\lambda)\n       \\longrightarrow(\\omega\\circ d)(x)\n       =\\omega(d(x))\n       \\qquad(\\omega\\in M_*).\n \\tag{N11}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d(x_\\lambda)\\to d(x)\\)\">\\(d(x_\\lambda)\\to d(x)\\)<\/span> ultraweakly. There is no interchange of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\)\">\\(t\\)<\/span>-limit with the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span>-net: (N10) first places each fixed pulled-back functional in the predual, and (N11) then uses that functional.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The general complex derivation.<\/strong> For an arbitrary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span>, L34 again gives boundedness. Set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n d^\\dagger(x)=d(x^*)^*,\\qquad\n d_1=\\frac{d+d^\\dagger}{2},\\qquad\n d_2=\\frac{d-d^\\dagger}{2i}.\n \\tag{N12}\n\\]\">\\[\n d^\\dagger(x)=d(x^*)^*,\\qquad\n d_1=\\frac{d+d^\\dagger}{2},\\qquad\n d_2=\\frac{d-d^\\dagger}{2i}.\n \\tag{N12}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe two conjugations make <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d^\\dagger\\)\">\\(d^\\dagger\\)<\/span> complex linear, and reversing the product twice gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n d^\\dagger(xy)\n       =x\\,d^\\dagger(y)+d^\\dagger(x)y.\n \\tag{N13}\n\\]\">\\[\n d^\\dagger(xy)\n       =x\\,d^\\dagger(y)+d^\\dagger(x)y.\n \\tag{N13}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus it is a derivation. Moreover\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((d^\\dagger)^\\dagger=d\\)\">\\((d^\\dagger)^\\dagger=d\\)<\/span>, and the dagger operation conjugates scalar coefficients. These identities give\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_j^\\dagger=d_j\\)\">\\(d_j^\\dagger=d_j\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j=1,2\\)\">\\(j=1,2\\)<\/span>; both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_j\\)\">\\(d_j\\)<\/span> are star derivations. The isometry of star also gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|d^\\dagger\\|=\\|d\\|\\)\">\\(\\|d^\\dagger\\|=\\|d\\|\\)<\/span>, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|d_j\\|\\leq\\|d\\|\\)\">\\(\\|d_j\\|\\leq\\|d\\|\\)<\/span>.\nThe star case makes both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_1\\)\">\\(d_1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_2\\)\">\\(d_2\\)<\/span> ultraweakly continuous. Their sum\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n d=d_1+i d_2\n \\tag{N14}\n\\]\">\\[\n d=d_1+i d_2\n \\tag{N14}\n\\]<\/span>\nis therefore ultraweakly continuous, proving the theorem. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The exponential product argument (N2)–(N5) also works in a possibly nonunital C*-algebra: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> denotes its identity linear map, and the statements involving an algebra identity are simply omitted. L34's boundedness theorem already covers that convention. The order and predual part above concerns the von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, with its own identity and its specified predual; the zero algebra was handled separately.<\/p>\n<p>This proof establishes normality before any inner implementer is chosen. The direct vector-series proof after the innerness theorem remains useful: once <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d(x)=kx-xk\\)\">\\(d(x)=kx-xk\\)<\/span> is known, it proves the same continuity by substituting vectors in the two multiplication maps. The present argument gives an independent order-and-predual route to that conclusion.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L35.md","start_offset":59147,"end_offset":66682,"label":null,"extraction_boundary":"all_numbered_statements_in_bound_passage"},"6280569990a6c560f37502ac1715c67a396b095aeadd78e4a9a0bbd8511edc6c":{"markdown":"**Theorem 2.1.** Products of Fredholm operators are Fredholm, and\n\\[\n\\kappa(ST)=\\kappa(S)+\\kappa(T).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]","html":"<p><strong>Theorem 2.1.<\/strong> Products of Fredholm operators are Fredholm, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\kappa(ST)=\\kappa(S)+\\kappa(T).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\kappa(ST)=\\kappa(S)+\\kappa(T).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FOUND-REMAINDER/src/fredholm-operators-and-stable-index.md","start_offset":8511,"end_offset":8626,"label":"Theorem 2.1","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"62c2e44564a70ecedea9f69d52d18a298ccb92923ffb2427874835b889c38235":{"markdown":"**Exercise 5.3 (further step).** Prove that \\(\\mathcal A\\) has a unique tracial state and that this state annihilates \\(\\mathcal K(H)\\). In particular, explain why the unique trace on \\(\\mathcal A\\) is not faithful, although the canonical traces on \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are faithful.","html":"<p><strong>Exercise 5.3 (further step).<\/strong> Prove that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A\\)<\/span> has a unique tracial state and that this state annihilates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal K(H)\\)\">\\(\\mathcal K(H)\\)<\/span>. In particular, explain why the unique trace on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A\\)<\/span> is not faithful, although the canonical traces on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L\\)\">\\(L\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R\\)\">\\(R\\)<\/span> are faithful.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FOUND-REMAINDER/src/free-group-averaging-and-the-compact-ideal.md","start_offset":22682,"end_offset":22962,"label":"Exercise 5.3","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"622dbc7f80c92c8697c1e2bf84776247b6b2beac80ea84e2c6edd9f4397bfef6":{"markdown":"**Lemma 3.2 (changing labels).** Tableaux of the same shape yield isomorphic representations. Their dimensions and normalization constants agree.","html":"<p><strong>Lemma 3.2 (changing labels).<\/strong> Tableaux of the same shape yield isomorphic representations. Their dimensions and normalization constants agree.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/RT-FIN/src/RT-FIN-13.md","start_offset":13472,"end_offset":13619,"label":"Lemma 3.2","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"62625b57304fbca8fdb9c6e47a5fc33f692ad325c4cf1e95dbb11dfb3542c610":{"markdown":"**Theorem 6.1** (Cauchy's theorem). Let \\(U\\subseteq\\mathbb C\\) be open and let \\(\\Gamma\\) be a cycle in \\(U\\) with \\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(\\alpha)=0\\) for every \\(\\alpha\\notin U\\). Let \\(g:U\\to X\\) be a map into a Banach space such that \\(\\varphi\\circ g\\) is holomorphic for every \\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\). Then \\(g\\) is continuous, \\(\\int_\\Gamma g(\\lambda)\\,d\\lambda=0\\), and\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\ng(z)\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)\\\\\n=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma\\frac{g(\\lambda)}{\\lambda-z}\\,d\\lambda\\\\\n(z\\in U\\setminus\\Gamma^*).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\n\nThis is Theorem 6.1(1)–(2) of the lesson on Cauchy's theorem. Its scalar case is Theorem 4.2 there. Applying a functional \\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\) reduces both formulas to the scalar case, and the Hahn–Banach theorem then gives them in \\(X\\).\n\nWe use the formula only at points of index \\(1\\) or \\(0\\). At a point \\(z\\) of index \\(0\\) it is the first conclusion, applied to \\(\\lambda\\mapsto g(\\lambda)/(\\lambda-z)\\) on \\(U\\setminus\\{z\\}\\). Applied on a disc, the formula gives the Taylor expansion of a holomorphic function at each point, in the usual way.","html":"<p><strong>Theorem 6.1<\/strong> (Cauchy's theorem). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\subseteq\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(U\\subseteq\\mathbb C\\)<\/span> be open and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span> be a cycle in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(\\alpha)=0\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(\\alpha)=0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\notin U\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\notin U\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g:U\\to X\\)\">\\(g:U\\to X\\)<\/span> be a map into a Banach space such that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\circ g\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\circ g\\)<\/span> is holomorphic for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span> is continuous, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int_\\Gamma g(\\lambda)\\,d\\lambda=0\\)\">\\(\\int_\\Gamma g(\\lambda)\\,d\\lambda=0\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\ng(z)\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)\\\\\n=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma\\frac{g(\\lambda)}{\\lambda-z}\\,d\\lambda\\\\\n(z\\in U\\setminus\\Gamma^*).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\ng(z)\\operatorname{Ind}_\\Gamma(z)\\\\\n=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\int_\\Gamma\\frac{g(\\lambda)}{\\lambda-z}\\,d\\lambda\\\\\n(z\\in U\\setminus\\Gamma^*).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>This is Theorem 6.1(1)–(2) of the lesson on Cauchy's theorem. Its scalar case is Theorem 4.2 there. Applying a functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\in X^*\\)<\/span> reduces both formulas to the scalar case, and the Hahn–Banach theorem then gives them in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>We use the formula only at points of index <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span>. At a point <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> of index <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span> it is the first conclusion, applied to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda\\mapsto g(\\lambda)/(\\lambda-z)\\)\">\\(\\lambda\\mapsto g(\\lambda)/(\\lambda-z)\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\setminus\\{z\\}\\)\">\\(U\\setminus\\{z\\}\\)<\/span>. Applied on a disc, the formula gives the Taylor expansion of a holomorphic function at each point, in the usual way.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/banach-algebras-spectrum-holomorphic-functional-calculus-and-gelfand-theory.md","start_offset":48562,"end_offset":49658,"label":"Theorem 6.1","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"62fd219467aaefbcb11c2d5e3b4a32c2a09bb39dcc160bbcdf8aa877ac354a02":{"markdown":"**Theorem 11.1** (Kuratowski–Ryll-Nardzewski). Let \\((\\Omega,\\mathcal S)\\) be a measurable space, \\(X\\) a Polish space, and \\(\\Phi\\) a map that assigns to each \\(\\omega\\in\\Omega\\) a nonempty closed set \\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\subseteq X\\), such that\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\{\\omega:\\ \\Phi(\\omega)\\cap U\\neq\\varnothing\\}\\in\\mathcal S\\\\\n\\text{for every open }U\\\\\n\\subseteq X.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{11.1}\n\\]\n1. For every open \\(U\\subseteq X\\) there is an \\(\\mathcal S\\)-measurable \\(f:\\Omega\\to X\\) with \\(f(\\omega)\\in\\Phi(\\omega)\\) for all \\(\\omega\\), and \\(f(\\omega)\\in U\\) whenever \\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\cap U\\neq\\varnothing\\).\n2. There are \\(\\mathcal S\\)-measurable maps \\(f_1,f_2,\\ldots:\\Omega\\to X\\) such that, for every \\(\\omega\\), all \\(f_n(\\omega)\\) lie in \\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\) and \\(\\{f_n(\\omega):n\\geq1\\}\\) is dense in \\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\).\n3. Conversely, if maps as in (2) exist, then \\(\\Phi\\) satisfies (11.1).","html":"<p><strong>Theorem 11.1<\/strong> (Kuratowski–Ryll-Nardzewski). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\Omega,\\mathcal S)\\)\">\\((\\Omega,\\mathcal S)\\)<\/span> be a measurable space, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> a Polish space, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi\\)\">\\(\\Phi\\)<\/span> a map that assigns to each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\in\\Omega\\)\">\\(\\omega\\in\\Omega\\)<\/span> a nonempty closed set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\subseteq X\\)\">\\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\subseteq X\\)<\/span>, such that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\{\\omega:\\ \\Phi(\\omega)\\cap U\\neq\\varnothing\\}\\in\\mathcal S\\\\\n\\text{for every open }U\\\\\n\\subseteq X.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{11.1}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\{\\omega:\\ \\Phi(\\omega)\\cap U\\neq\\varnothing\\}\\in\\mathcal S\\\\\n\\text{for every open }U\\\\\n\\subseteq X.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{11.1}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>For every open <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\subseteq X\\)\">\\(U\\subseteq X\\)<\/span> there is an <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal S\\)\">\\(\\mathcal S\\)<\/span>-measurable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f:\\Omega\\to X\\)\">\\(f:\\Omega\\to X\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(\\omega)\\in\\Phi(\\omega)\\)\">\\(f(\\omega)\\in\\Phi(\\omega)\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\)\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(\\omega)\\in U\\)\">\\(f(\\omega)\\in U\\)<\/span> whenever <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\cap U\\neq\\varnothing\\)\">\\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\cap U\\neq\\varnothing\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>There are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal S\\)\">\\(\\mathcal S\\)<\/span>-measurable maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_1,f_2,\\ldots:\\Omega\\to X\\)\">\\(f_1,f_2,\\ldots:\\Omega\\to X\\)<\/span> such that, for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\)\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span>, all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_n(\\omega)\\)\">\\(f_n(\\omega)\\)<\/span> lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\)\">\\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{f_n(\\omega):n\\geq1\\}\\)\">\\(\\{f_n(\\omega):n\\geq1\\}\\)<\/span> is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\)\">\\(\\Phi(\\omega)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>Conversely, if maps as in (2) exist, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Phi\\)\">\\(\\Phi\\)<\/span> satisfies (11.1).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/numerical-ranges-positive-matrices-and-co-souslin-sets.md","start_offset":98198,"end_offset":99091,"label":"Theorem 11.1","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"62127c2ab181d070c05da62c6427a10f90e94301109cb6e5581f9b3845b0af1b":{"markdown":"**Example 6.6** (The parts must be clopen). In \\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\) the isolated points are the points of \\(\\mathbb N\\), so \\(\\Omega_d=\\beta\\mathbb N\\) and \\(\\Omega_c=\\varnothing\\). The partition into \\(A=\\mathbb N\\) and \\(B=\\beta\\mathbb N\\setminus\\mathbb N\\) also has the two properties of Proposition 6.5(2), except that the parts are not clopen. Clearly \\(\\mathbb N\\) is open, discrete and dense in itself. To see that \\(B\\) has no isolated points, let \\(p\\in B\\) and let \\(C\\) be a clopen neighbourhood of \\(p\\) in \\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\). The set \\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\) is infinite: otherwise \\(C=\\overline{C\\cap\\mathbb N}\\), which holds because \\(\\mathbb N\\) is dense and \\(C\\) is open, would be a finite subset of \\(\\mathbb N\\) and could not contain \\(p\\). Split \\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\) into two disjoint infinite sets \\(N_1,N_2\\). They are open, so their closures are disjoint (Lemma 4.2(1)) and lie in \\(C\\). The closure of an infinite subset of \\(\\mathbb N\\) is compact and so is not a subset of the discrete set \\(\\mathbb N\\); hence each closure contains a point of \\(B\\). So \\(C\\cap B\\) has at least two points, and \\(p\\) is not isolated in \\(B\\).","html":"<p><strong>Example 6.6<\/strong> (The parts must be clopen). In <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> the isolated points are the points of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d=\\beta\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d=\\beta\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_c=\\varnothing\\)\">\\(\\Omega_c=\\varnothing\\)<\/span>. The partition into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(A=\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=\\beta\\mathbb N\\setminus\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(B=\\beta\\mathbb N\\setminus\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> also has the two properties of Proposition 6.5(2), except that the parts are not clopen. Clearly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> is open, discrete and dense in itself. To see that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> has no isolated points, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\in B\\)\">\\(p\\in B\\)<\/span> and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> be a clopen neighbourhood of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\)<\/span>. The set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> is infinite: otherwise <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C=\\overline{C\\cap\\mathbb N}\\)\">\\(C=\\overline{C\\cap\\mathbb N}\\)<\/span>, which holds because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> is dense and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> is open, would be a finite subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> and could not contain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>. Split <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> into two disjoint infinite sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_1,N_2\\)\">\\(N_1,N_2\\)<\/span>. They are open, so their closures are disjoint (Lemma 4.2(1)) and lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>. The closure of an infinite subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> is compact and so is not a subset of the discrete set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span>; hence each closure contains a point of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\cap B\\)\">\\(C\\cap B\\)<\/span> has at least two points, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> is not isolated in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/abelian-operator-algebras.md","start_offset":97605,"end_offset":98750,"label":"Example 6.6","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"620c41eef53d9e809c31665284030e6ef58884772ae6c851dab5edc80661b852":{"markdown":"**Example 18.3** (A normal state that is not a vector state). Let \\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\) on \\(\\mathbb C^2\\) and \\(\\varphi=\\frac12\\mathrm{tr}\\). Its support is \\(1\\). Each vector functional \\(\\omega_\\xi\\) has support \\(p_\\xi\\), the rank-one projection onto \\(\\mathbb C\\xi\\) (here \\(M'=\\mathbb C1\\)). So \\(\\varphi\\) is not a vector functional, in line with Proposition 18.2: \\(s(\\varphi)=1\\not\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\) for every \\(\\xi_0\\). Accordingly \\(M\\) has no separating vector. On \\(\\mathbb C^2\\otimes\\mathbb C^2\\), with \\(M\\) acting as \\(M\\otimes1\\), the vector \\((\\delta_1\\otimes\\delta_1+\\delta_2\\otimes\\delta_2)/\\sqrt2\\) is separating and gives \\(\\varphi\\).","html":"<p><strong>Example 18.3<\/strong> (A normal state that is not a vector state). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi=\\frac12\\mathrm{tr}\\)\">\\(\\varphi=\\frac12\\mathrm{tr}\\)<\/span>. Its support is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>. Each vector functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega_\\xi\\)\">\\(\\omega_\\xi\\)<\/span> has support <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_\\xi\\)\">\\(p_\\xi\\)<\/span>, the rank-one projection onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C\\xi\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C\\xi\\)<\/span> (here <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M&#x27;=\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(M&#x27;=\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span>). So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> is not a vector functional, in line with Proposition 18.2: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s(\\varphi)=1\\not\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)\">\\(s(\\varphi)=1\\not\\precsim p_{\\xi_0}\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi_0\\)\">\\(\\xi_0\\)<\/span>. Accordingly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> has no separating vector. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C^2\\otimes\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C^2\\otimes\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> acting as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\otimes1\\)\">\\(M\\otimes1\\)<\/span>, the vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\delta_1\\otimes\\delta_1+\\delta_2\\otimes\\delta_2)/\\sqrt2\\)\">\\((\\delta_1\\otimes\\delta_1+\\delta_2\\otimes\\delta_2)/\\sqrt2\\)<\/span> is separating and gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/projections-and-types-of-von-neumann-algebras.md","start_offset":134244,"end_offset":134903,"label":"Example 18.3","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"6276008c813bbadb1ee3027d2b5ebfa79a5242710abbd54204a6069bacca4e49":{"markdown":"**Theorem 10.2** (Weak compactness in \\(M_*\\)). For a subset \\(K\\) of the predual of a von Neumann algebra \\(M\\), the following are equivalent.\n\n1. \\(K\\) is relatively weakly compact.\n2. For every abelian von Neumann subalgebra \\(\\mathcal A\\subseteq M\\), the set \\(K|_{\\mathcal A}=\\{\\varphi|_{\\mathcal A}:\\varphi\\in K\\}\\) is relatively weakly compact in \\(\\mathcal A_*\\).\n3. \\(K\\) is bounded, and \\(\\varphi(p_n)\\to0\\) uniformly in \\(\\varphi\\in K\\) whenever the projections \\(p_n\\) decrease to \\(0\\).\n4. \\(K\\) is bounded, and there is \\(\\omega\\in M_*^+\\) with this property: for every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) there is \\(\\delta>0\\) such that \\(|\\varphi(a)|<\\varepsilon\\) for all \\(\\varphi\\in K\\) whenever \\(a\\in M_1\\) and \\(\\omega(a^*a+aa^*)<\\delta\\).\n5. \\(K\\) is bounded, and for every increasing net of projections \\((p_i)\\), \\(\\varphi(p_i)\\) converges uniformly in \\(\\varphi\\in K\\).\n6. \\(K\\) is bounded, and \\(\\|(1-p_i)\\varphi(1-p_i)\\|\\to0\\) uniformly in \\(\\varphi\\in K\\) for every increasing net of projections \\((p_i)\\) with \\(\\sup_ip_i=1\\).\n7. \\(K\\) is bounded, and \\(\\varphi(q_n)\\to0\\) uniformly in \\(\\varphi\\in K\\) for every sequence \\((q_n)\\) of mutually orthogonal projections.\n\n\nWe prove the easy implications first; the implication (1)\\(\\Rightarrow\\)(4) needs two lemmas.","html":"<p><strong>Theorem 10.2<\/strong> (Weak compactness in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span>). For a subset <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> of the predual of a von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, the following are equivalent.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is relatively weakly compact.<\/li>\n<li>For every abelian von Neumann subalgebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A\\subseteq M\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A\\subseteq M\\)<\/span>, the set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K|_{\\mathcal A}=\\{\\varphi|_{\\mathcal A}:\\varphi\\in K\\}\\)\">\\(K|_{\\mathcal A}=\\{\\varphi|_{\\mathcal A}:\\varphi\\in K\\}\\)<\/span> is relatively weakly compact in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A_*\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A_*\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is bounded, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(p_n)\\to0\\)\">\\(\\varphi(p_n)\\to0\\)<\/span> uniformly in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\in K\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\in K\\)<\/span> whenever the projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p_n\\)\">\\(p_n\\)<\/span> decrease to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is bounded, and there is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\in M_*^+\\)\">\\(\\omega\\in M_*^+\\)<\/span> with this property: for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)<\/span> there is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\delta&gt;0\\)<\/span> such that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|\\varphi(a)|&lt;\\varepsilon\\)\">\\(|\\varphi(a)|&lt;\\varepsilon\\)<\/span> for all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\in K\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\in K\\)<\/span> whenever <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in M_1\\)\">\\(a\\in M_1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega(a^*a+aa^*)&lt;\\delta\\)\">\\(\\omega(a^*a+aa^*)&lt;\\delta\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is bounded, and for every increasing net of projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((p_i)\\)\">\\((p_i)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(p_i)\\)\">\\(\\varphi(p_i)\\)<\/span> converges uniformly in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\in K\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\in K\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is bounded, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|(1-p_i)\\varphi(1-p_i)\\|\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|(1-p_i)\\varphi(1-p_i)\\|\\to0\\)<\/span> uniformly in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\in K\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\in K\\)<\/span> for every increasing net of projections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((p_i)\\)\">\\((p_i)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sup_ip_i=1\\)\">\\(\\sup_ip_i=1\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span> is bounded, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi(q_n)\\to0\\)\">\\(\\varphi(q_n)\\to0\\)<\/span> uniformly in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\in K\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\in K\\)<\/span> for every sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((q_n)\\)\">\\((q_n)\\)<\/span> of mutually orthogonal projections.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>We prove the easy implications first; the implication (1)<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Rightarrow\\)\">\\(\\Rightarrow\\)<\/span>(4) needs two lemmas.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/polar-decomposition-of-functionals-and-weak-compactness-in-preduals.md","start_offset":99401,"end_offset":100678,"label":"Theorem 10.2","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"},"62d2abd9798671329c3754d2d3a720b1e25d194753dee42db9f84e49e5c40951":{"markdown":"**Theorem 3.1.** The following are equivalent:\n\n1. \\(L^2(G/K)\\) is multiplicity free.\n2. \\(\\dim V_\\pi^K\\leq1\\) for every irreducible \\(\\pi\\).\n3. \\(\\mathcal A\\) is commutative.\n\nA pair with these properties is a **Gelfand pair**. Multiplicity free allows irreducibles to occur zero times.","html":"<p><strong>Theorem 3.1.<\/strong> The following are equivalent:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(G/K)\\)\">\\(L^2(G/K)\\)<\/span> is multiplicity free.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\dim V_\\pi^K\\leq1\\)\">\\(\\dim V_\\pi^K\\leq1\\)<\/span> for every irreducible <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi\\)\">\\(\\pi\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal A\\)\">\\(\\mathcal A\\)<\/span> is commutative.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>A pair with these properties is a <strong>Gelfand pair<\/strong>. Multiplicity free allows irreducibles to occur zero times.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/representations-of-compact-groups/courses/representations-of-compact-groups/src/RT-CPT-13.md","start_offset":5746,"end_offset":6035,"label":"Theorem 3.1","extraction_boundary":"numbered_statement_boundary"}});
